---

<Base>
  <header class="mast">
    <a class="brand" href="/"><span class="sym">∇</span> Nabla</a>
    <nav>
      <a href="/" aria-current="page">Notes</a>
      <a href="/play">Play</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/sr2echa/Nabla" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub</a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <article class="paper">
    <h1><span class="sym">∇</span> Nabla</h1>
    <p class="lede">Nabla is a chess engine that chooses moves by energy descent on ray-transport fields.</p>
    <p><a class="go" href="/play">Try it out <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></p>

    <h2>The rays</h2>
    <p>The science here is ray transport, in the sense of the Beer-Lambert law. A sliding piece sends influence along each ray it can travel. The direct channel is exactly the squares it attacks, stopping at the first piece in the way. A second channel, the X-ray, attenuates past blockers according to what stands there — nearly transparent through a battery mate, partial through enemy pieces, and nearly opaque through enemy pawns — stopping once transmittance falls below the cutoff. Knights, kings, and pawns are single steps, not long rays.</p>
    <p>An electrostatic picture of the board was tried and was not kept. Permittivity let influence pass through enemy pieces and treated every friendly blocker the same. The model that remains is the two-channel ray.</p>
    <h2>Why chess</h2>
    <p>The long-range pieces already move on rays, and the words players use for a position (a file, a pin, a battery, control of a square) are statements about those rays. Nabla turns the rays into two fields for each side, then into one energy, white-positive and in pawns. It plays the legal move that raises its own energy the most. The point is that the reason for a move can be read back as those fields, term by term, rather than as a number from a search.</p>

    <h2>The energy</h2>
    <p>Every position has one number, <em>E</em>. It is white-positive and measured in pawns. Positive means the position favours White. The number is a sum of nine terms, equation (11):</p>
    <p class="math"></p>
    <p>The terms are not all the same size in an endgame as they are in a middlegame. A phase, <em>φ</em>, moves the weights between those two regimes. Equation (12):</p>
    <p class="math"></p>
    <p>Turn the board around, swap the colours, and the number changes sign. That is colour antisymmetry, equation (27), and it holds bit for bit:</p>
    <p class="math"></p>
    <p><em>E</em> depends only on the position in front of it. Checkmate, stalemate, and repetition are boundary conditions on the move, not extra terms in the sum.</p>

    <h2>The nine terms</h2>
    <dl>
      <div><dt>Material</dt><dd>Who has more wood. A pawn is 1, and the other pieces are multiples of that pawn.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Mobility</dt><dd>Safe squares a piece can use. A ray stops at the first piece that blocks it.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Territory</dt><dd>Who bears on the board. The centre counts more than the edge.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Exchange</dt><dd>Whether a capture wins material. One move cannot both take and defend.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>King</dt><dd>Enemy influence on the king, held down by a pawn shelter.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Tropism</dt><dd>A weak pull of the pieces toward the enemy king.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Pawns</dt><dd>Passed, isolated, and doubled pawns, and whether a passer can be caught.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Confinement</dt><dd>In an ending, how small a region the losing king can still reach.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Tempo</dt><dd>A tenth of a pawn for having the move.</dd></div>
    </dl>

    <h2>How a move is chosen</h2>
    <p>Nabla evaluates the legal moves by steepest descent of the mover's potential <em>V</em><sub>mover</sub> = −<em>e</em> (equivalently raising its own energy <em>e</em> the most, toward lower energy for the opponent).</p>
    <p>Level 0 scores the move itself, and the forced replies if the move gives check. Level 1 also plays out the captures that follow. Level 2 looks at one full reply, then the captures. That is a little lookahead, not a deep search. The seats use Level 2 unless you change them. How long a decision takes is in the next section.</p>
    <p>Temperature spreads the choice. At 0, Nabla plays the best move it found. A higher temperature sometimes plays a lesser one. The cap is 5 pawns. Each colour can have its own level. You set both from that colour's seat.</p>

    <h2>The model</h2>
    <p>Nabla is not a trained network. The energy is 57 named coefficients (parameter hash <code>c3deee9880eb64dc</code>), the ones in the design notes, plus four constants that stay fixed: a pawn is the unit of energy, a ray stops once its transmittance falls below 0.05, the light cone has sharpness 0.1, and the control scale is 1. The coefficients in this version are the hand-set starting values; <code>nabla-tune</code> can print a fit on quiet positions and does not replace them.</p>
    <p>The page and the command-line engine are the same program. The page's copy is WebAssembly, committed as <code>web/src/engine/nabla_wasm_bg.wasm</code> (935,057 bytes, checked 21 November 2026; 48/48 native-vs-WASM bench decisions bit-identical). Building the site does not compile Rust again.</p>
    <p>Choosing a move costs one visit per position it looks at. On an Intel Core i7-7700HQ, one thread, the 16-position bench reaches roughly 120k nps at Level 2 (about 8.3 microseconds per visit). Level 0 visits each legal move and the forced replies when the move gives check. Level 2, the default in a seat, also plays one reply and the captures after it, so a sharp position costs more than a quiet one.</p>
    <p>Three positions from that bench, Level 2, temperature 0, no clock, on that machine (7-run medians), 21 November 2026:</p>
    <table class="results">
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Starting position</td><td>421 visits, 2 ms</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Middlegame, all pieces still on</td><td>5,889 visits, 46 ms</td></tr>
        <tr><td>King and one pawn</td><td>50 visits, under 1 ms</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The middlegame is the sharp one: the captures run sixteen plies deep. The same three positions at Level 0 stayed under a millisecond (20, 50, and 6 visits). Across all sixteen bench positions (5-run native medians), decision latency is 0.36 ms at Level 0, 12.54 ms at Level 1, and 21.02 ms at Level 2.</p>

    <ModelLatencyChart client:load data={modelLatencyData} />

    <h2>Rules and scope</h2>
    <p>The engine follows standard FIDE chess rules via <code>shakmaty</code>: castling on both wings, en passant (with SEE removing the captured pawn before X-ray discovery), all four pawn promotions (queen promotions in quiescence), checkmate, stalemate, the fifty-move rule, insufficient material, threefold repetition on the board, and first-recurrence repetition (&plusmn;0.15 pawn contempt) during search. Chess960 is excluded.</p>
    <p>Only territory and king pressure read the ray field <em>Φ</em><sub><em>c</em></sub>; the other seven terms use exact bitboards and SEE. Static <em>E</em>(<em>S</em>) omits castling rights, SEE ignores pins, and the hand-set weights sit at MAE 1.79 pawns against Stockfish depth 10. Recomputing the field costs ~8.3 µs per node (~15× slower per node than classical piece-square tables), so Nabla leads PeSTO alpha-beta at equal search depth (+844 Elo at 1-ply, +56 Elo at 2-ply), while deeper classical search wins at equal wall-clock time.</p>

    <h2>What the board shows</h2>
    <p>The play page draws the position, not a second board of ornaments. A steel rim means White controls that square more than Black. An olive rim means the opposite. The rim is drawn only where the difference is clear, so an empty centre stays an empty square.</p>
    <p>Corner ticks mean both sides bear on the square. A number in the corner is the pawns the opponent wins by taking the piece there. An arrow is a move about to be played. A paler arrow is another move that was considered. The question mark on the play page opens a small board with each of these marks, and the switches that turn them on.</p>
    <p>The bar to the left of the board is a reading of the position. The cream end is the side White's pieces are on, and the grey end is Black's, including after a flip. A mark sits at the midpoint. Nabla's reading is a logistic of the white-positive energy, with scale 0.4. It is not a measured win rate. Positive is White ahead. Stockfish lite can read the same position in pawns, from White's side, and a mate it sees is written M2 or −M4. The score and the switch between those readings sit under the board.</p>
    <p>The column on the right is Nabla's reading of the position, and it stays while the other side thinks. Two Nablas use two colours. Pause the game and hover a mark on the board to see the pieces and squares behind it. The delay before an engine move is at the top of a seat. The game waits while that seat is open.</p>

    <h2>Benchmarks</h2>
    <p>Measured 21 November 2026 on native builds using <code>fastchess</code> on an Intel Core i7-7700HQ across <strong>11,920 games</strong> (9,520 tournament games plus 2,400 endgame conversion games; zero adjudication; 0 time forfeits, 0 illegal moves, 0 crashes; 40/40 UCI compliance). Joint <code>Ordo</code> v1.0 ratings run over 4,780 pool games on <code>8moves_v3.pgn</code> at 10+0.1 s, pinned to Stockfish 19 <code>UCI_LimitStrength</code> anchors (1320/1500/1700/1900). Browser play uses the same WASM core, while full protocol tables live in the <a href="https://github.com/sr2echa/Nabla/blob/main/docs/research/benchmark-report.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">benchmark report on GitHub</a>.</p>

    <EloLadderChart client:load data={eloLadderData} />

    <ComputeParetoChart client:load data={eloLadderData} />

    <p>Across pairwise checks, component ablations, temperature sweeps, tactical puzzles, endgame conversions, and quiet evaluation probes, the strengths and open gaps separate cleanly:</p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="results wide">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Measurement</th>
            <th>Observed result</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Level scaling</td><td>L0→L1 +443 &plusmn; 93 Elo; L1→L2 +372 &plusmn; 79 Elo (<code>noob_3moves</code>: +419 &plusmn; 79)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>vs Stockfish 19</td><td>L2 vs SF-1500: 58–40–2 (+63 &plusmn; 65, 53× fewer nodes, 20× less time); vs SF-1320: +220 &plusmn; 80; vs SF-1700: −131 &plusmn; 78</td></tr>
          <tr><td>vs Maia &amp; PeSTO</td><td>L2 beats Maia-1100/1500/1900 (+359 / +193 / +151 Elo); L0 +798 &plusmn; 198 vs AB-PeSTO-0; L2 +56 &plusmn; 45 vs AB-PeSTO-2</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Ablations (3,200 games)</td><td>L0: Exchange −920, Pawns −91, <em>λ</em> = 0 −44; L1: Pawns −70, King −60, Exchange −53; untuned <em>χ</em> = 0 within noise</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Temperature &amp; blunders</td><td>T = 0.10 → 2.00 monotonic drop, entropy 1.50 → 2.79 nats, blunders 8.2% → 20.2%; L2 (T = 0) ACPL 67.1, blunders 7.8% vs Maia-1500 10.5%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tactics (WAC-300 / BK-24)</td><td>L2: 85/300 (28.3%) and 5/24 (20.8%), vs AB-PeSTO-2 30.7% / 12.5% and Maia-1500 13.0% / 16.7%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Endgames vs SF d8 (<em>n</em> = 200)</td><td>L2 with vs without confinement: KQK 95.0% vs 59.5%, KRK 48.5% vs 7.5%, KPK 74.5% vs 38.5%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Static eval vs SF d10 (<em>n</em> = 2,000)</td><td>Spearman <em>ρ</em> = 0.865, Pearson <em>r</em> = 0.907, sign agreement 87.5%, MAE 1.79 pawns (Middlegame <em>ρ</em> = 0.979)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Stability &amp; WASM parity</td><td>0 time losses across 9,520 games; 40/40 UCI compliance; 48/48 native–WASM bit-identical (1.42× at L2)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <DiagnosticsCharts
      client:load
      ablations={ablationsData}
      temperature={temperatureData}
      tacticsEndgames={tacticsEndgamesData}
      staticEval={staticEvalData}
    />

    <p>Stockfish's Elo cap, when you play it in the lab, is Stockfish's own limit.</p>

